# DTF tier manifest: push-sync-native-formats  (artifact-keeper#4725, fixed by #4779, 1.11.3)
# Profile-set: filesystem + a real second Artifact Keeper instance
# (peer.second-instance: `peer-backend` with its own `peer-db`, same image,
# published on 127.0.0.1:${TRIVY_PORT}). Instance A is `backend`; B is the
# push peer A's sync worker delivers to over the slot's compose network.
#
# THE BUG (#4725)
# Push and mirror peers only receive a hosted upload that queues a push
# sync_tasks row. #4643/#4644 (1.11.2) wired Maven, npm, Go and the generic
# REST upload; twenty-six other native-format handlers write their artifact
# rows themselves (raw INSERT, proxy_helpers::insert_artifact or a local
# upsert) and never queued a row, so a client publish in those formats only
# reached a peer after a manual run-now sync. #4779 calls
# queue_push_sync_tasks_for_upload in every one of them; the helper skips the
# fan-out when the request carries X-Artifact-Keeper-Replication: true, so a
# replicated write is never pushed back out.
#
# THE PEER
# The oracle mints a `*`-scoped API key on B, registers B on A
# (POST /api/v1/peers, endpoint http://peer-backend:8080), sets it online in
# A's DB (instead of waiting up to 60 s for the first heartbeat probe, which
# B then answers), and subscribes four hosted repos in push mode. The same
# repo keys exist on B. Nothing about the transfer is simulated: A's sync
# worker (10 s tick) claims the rows and PUTs the bytes to B's
# /api/v1/repositories/{key}/artifacts/{path}.
#
# Native protocols used (their upload paths are not the generic REST path):
#   bazel        PUT  /bazel/{repo}/modules/dtfsync/1.0.0/MODULE.bazel
#   Git LFS      PUT  /lfs/{repo}/objects/{oid}
#   Hugging Face POST /huggingface/{repo}/api/models/{model}/upload/main
# (Maven, npm and Go already queued on 1.11.2, so they cannot discriminate.)
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL) true on BOTH images: all uploads succeed and their rows carry
#             the uploaded sha256; the generic REST upload queues one push
#             row and the worker delivers it to B (row on B, same sha256, B
#             serves the bytes, A's row completed); a bazel upload carrying
#             X-Artifact-Keeper-Replication stores, queues nothing and never
#             reaches B.
#   (FIX)     RED on 1.11.2: each native upload queues exactly one push row
#             on A (DB); each reaches B within the generic control's delivery
#             plus a 25 s grace (row on B with the same sha256, B serves the
#             bytes at the native path); A's GET /api/v1/peers/{id}/sync/tasks
#             is empty, the peer is online with last_sync_at set, and all four
#             push rows are completed.
#
# Teardown note: `harness/run.sh down --slot N` only knows the --<dimension>
# profiles, not this one. A tier run without --keep tears B down itself; after
# --keep, use `docker compose -p ak-dtf<N> -f compose.base.yml
# -f profiles/peer.second-instance.yml down -v`.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: ~30 authenticated calls back to back on A and B.
# UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=true: the peer endpoint goes through
# validate_outbound_url and B sits on a private compose address.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem peer.second-instance"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
